{% set name = "Universal Resolver" %}
{% set logo = "sketching" %}
{% set subtitle = "Community DID Infrastructure" %}
{% set repoTag = "" %}

{% extends "default.html.njk" %}
<!-- {% block svg %}

{% endblock %} -->

{% block content %}
  <section class="page-title theme-bg">
    <div class="container">
      <svg>
        <use xlink:href="/images/icons.svg#{{ logo }}"></use>
      </svg>
      <h1>{{ name }}</h1>
      {% if subtitle %}
        <h3>{{ subtitle }}</h3>
      {% else %}
        <h3>Decentralized Identity Foundation</h3>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="counter-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="row justify-content-md-center">
        <div class="col-12 col-lg-6">
          <div class="section-title text-center">
            <h2 class="font-alt">A Successful Incubation</h2>
            <div class="title-border">
              <span class="lg"></span>
              <span class="md"></span>
              <span class="sm"></span>
            </div>
            <br>
            <p>
              As of October 14, 2026, The Decentralized Identity Foundation is no longer hosting a "community staging 
              server" for the Universal Resolver. The complexity of hosting and administering it as a public resource has 
              grown over the years, and the number of production-grade Universal Resolver service providers
              (with free tiers) have largely obviated the need for DIF to "seed" the market, which is growing into 
              maturity.
            </p>
            <p>
              DID-curious developers are encouraged to check out one of the two member-hosted services listed below for 
              experimentation and early research, to self-host (and contribute) to the open-source work items powering them, 
              and to consider DIF-affiliated commercial options for production-grade infrastructural needs. Both deployments below are 
              deployed and funded by DIF members, who maintain at DIF the respective reference implementations they are based on. 
              both. Some of these drivers, like those for 
              <a href=https://github.com/decentralized-identity/ethr-did-resolver><code>did:ethr</code></a> and 
              <a href=https://github.com/decentralized-identity/uni-resolver-driver-did-webvh><code>did:webvh</code></a>,
              are also DIF work items in their own right, and thus good examples to analyze if you are planning your own driver contribution.
           </p>
           <p>
             In historical order, the main DIF-member deployments are:
           </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

<section class="counter-section gray-bg">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="row justify-content-md-center">
        <div class="col-12 col-md-6 col-lg-3">
          <div class="section-title text-center">
            <h2 class="font-alt">GoDiddy Universal Resolver, hosted by DanubeTech</h2>
            <p>
              <i>Productionized reference implementation of the original Universal Resolver form-factor.</i>
            </p>
            <div class="title-border">
              <span class="lg"></span>
              <span class="md"></span>
              <span class="sm"></span>
            </div>
            <br>
            <p>
             In addition to the more full-featured <a href="https://godiddy.com">GoDiddy</a> product, DanubeTech hosts an instance of the
             classic white-label Universal Resolver interface at <a href="https://uniresolver.io">uniresolver.io</a>, 
             against which self-hosters can compare and test their own deployments of the 
             <a href="https://github.com/decentralized-identity/universal-resolver">Universal Resolver</a> reference implementation,
             developed at DIF and continuously maintained by the community with over 100 contributors and counting.
             It is comprised of a workhorse Java router and Docker containers to support the widest possible range of DID methods. 
             </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="col-12 col-md-6 col-lg-3>
          <div class="section-title text-center">
            <h2 class="font-alt">ThisDID, by GoPlausible</h2>
            <p>
              <i>Edge-worker-optimized and proxy-fallback wrapper around DIF's NPM-based resolver engine</i>
            </p>
            <div class="title-border">
              <span class="lg"></span>
              <span class="md"></span>
              <span class="sm"></span>
            </div>
            <br>
            <p>
            <a href="https://thisdid.com">ThisDID.com</a>, is a sample deployment of the recent 
            <a href="https://github.com/decentralized-identity/universal-resolver-ts">TypeScript/NPM
            re-implementation of the Universal Resolver</a>, applying an alternate architectural approach.
            It uses an "edge-worker" deployment pattern to optimize for low latency and operational cost for methods which 
            have been packaged as NPM packages (using the 
            <a href="https://github.com/decentralized-identity/did-resolver">DID resolver NPM API and template</a> designed and 
            maintained by DIF's <a href="https://veramo.io/">Veramo</a> project). It "falls back" to proxy resolution for all other 
            drivers, load-balancing (and ranking) where multiple backends support these non-NPM resolution drivers.
             </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Blog End -->
{% endblock %}
{% block scripts %}
  {{ super() }}
  <script src="/js/education.js"></script>
{% endblock %}
